@page "/getting-started"

<PageTitle>Getting started · RioCharts for Blazor and .NET</PageTitle>

<h1>Getting started</h1>
<p class="lead">
    RioCharts draws the same chart in a Blazor app, as PNG or SVG on the server, from a JSON definition, or from an AI agent. Pick where
    you start; the chart model is the same everywhere.
</p>

<h2 id="paths">Choose your starting point</h2>

<h3>In a Blazor app</h3>
<p>
    Interactive charts with tooltips, zoom and keyboard access, in Server, WebAssembly, Auto, static rendering or MAUI Blazor Hybrid.
    Add <code>RioCharts.Blazor</code>, <code>@@using RioCharts.Blazor</code> and <code>@@using RioCharts.Core</code>, and a chart; the
    <a href="getting-started#blazor">Blazor section</a> below goes on from there.
</p>
<CodeBlock Code="@BlazorPathCode" />

<h3>Images on the server</h3>
<p>
    PNG and SVG from a background job, an API endpoint or a report, with no browser. Add <code>RioCharts.Skia</code>, describe the chart
    with a <code>ChartDefinition</code> and pass the data as columns. See <a href="server-export">Server-side export</a>.
</p>
<CodeBlock Language="csharp" Code="@ServerPathCode" />

<h3>From JSON</h3>
<p>
    Save charts as JSON, let users edit them, or store them with a dashboard. A definition names a dataset and its fields; it renders in
    Blazor with <code>&lt;RioChart Definition Datasets&gt;</code> and on the server with RioCharts.Skia. Try the
    <a href="definitions">playground</a>.
</p>
<CodeBlock Language="csharp" Code="@JsonPathCode" />

<h3>From AI agents</h3>
<p>
    Let a model chart your data: <code>RioCharts.AI</code> gives your own agent <code>create_chart</code> and <code>render_chart</code>
    tools (Microsoft.Extensions.AI or MCP), and <code>riocharts-mcp</code> lets local agents such as Claude Code or Copilot chart CSV and
    JSON files. See <a href="ai">AI agents and MCP</a>.
</p>
<CodeBlock Language="csharp" Code="@AiPathCode" />

<h2 id="packages">Packages</h2>
<p>
    RioCharts is on <a href="https://www.nuget.org/packages?q=RioCharts" target="_blank" rel="noopener">NuGet</a>.
    Take the parts you need; each brings in what it depends on. The packages are release candidates until 1.0, so add <code>--prerelease</code>
    (or tick <em>Include prerelease</em> in Visual Studio). All target .NET 10 (LTS) and .NET 11; <code>riocharts-mcp</code> runs on either.
</p>
<table class="docs">
    <tr><th>Package</th><th>For</th></tr>
    @foreach (var p in Packages)
    {
        <tr>
            <td><a href="@($"https://www.nuget.org/packages/{p.Id}")" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><code>@p.Id</code></a></td>
            <td>@p.Text</td>
        </tr>
    }
</table>
<CodeBlock Language="shell" Code="@InstallCode" />
<p>
    <code>riocharts-mcp</code> is a .NET tool rather than a library; see <a href="ai#local-agents">AI agents and MCP</a>.
    Update it with <code>dotnet tool update -g RioCharts.Mcp --prerelease</code>.
</p>

<h2 id="blazor">Blazor apps</h2>
<p>For Blazor (Server, WebAssembly, Auto or <a href="getting-started#render-modes">MAUI Blazor Hybrid</a>), add one package:</p>
<CodeBlock Language="shell" Code="dotnet add package RioCharts.Blazor --prerelease" />
<p>Then in <code>_Imports.razor</code>:</p>
<CodeBlock Code="@ImportsCode" />
<p>Styles come with your app's <code>{App}.styles.css</code> bundle automatically; there is nothing else to register. Zero configuration gives a finished chart.</p>
<h3 id="coding-agents">Using a coding agent</h3>
<p>
    Models do not know RioCharts yet. Paste this into your repository's <code>AGENTS.md</code> (or <code>CLAUDE.md</code>,
    <code>.github/copilot-instructions.md</code>) so the agent reads the guide before it writes chart code.
    <a href="llms-full.txt" target="_blank"><code>llms-full.txt</code></a> has setup, rules, examples and every parameter in one file;
    see also <a href="ai#coding-agents">coding agents and MCP</a>.
</p>
<CodeBlock Language="markdown" Code="@AgentsCode" />

<Example Title="Your first chart" Code="@FirstCode">
    <Description>
        <p>Pass items and say which property is x and which is y. The axis type follows the data: times give a time axis, numbers a linear axis, strings a category axis.</p>
    </Description>
    <ChildContent>
        <RioChart Items="DailyOrders">
            <LineSeries XValue="r => r.Time" YValue="r => r.Value" />
        </RioChart>
    </ChildContent>
</Example>

<Example Title="Several series" Code="@SeveralCode">
    <Description>
        <p>More series add a legend. Click a legend item to hide a series; the axis refits and colors stay put.</p>
    </Description>
    <ChildContent>
        <RioChart TItem="Reading" YAxis="Sessions">
            <LineSeries Items="WebSessions" Name="Web" XValue="r => r.Time" YValue="r => r.Value" />
            <LineSeries Items="AppSessions" Name="App" XValue="r => r.Time" YValue="r => r.Value" />
        </RioChart>
    </ChildContent>
</Example>

<Example Title="Arrays instead of items" Code="@ArraysCode">
    <Description>
        <p>For large data, pass columns directly with <code>SeriesData.From</code>: no per-item cost. The chart then needs some <code>TItem</code>, e.g. <code>double</code>.</p>
    </Description>
    <ChildContent>
        <RioChart TItem="double" Height="240">
            <LineSeries Name="Response time" Data="_columns" StrokeWidth="1.25" />
        </RioChart>
    </ChildContent>
</Example>

<Example Title="Shared defaults" Code="@DefaultsCode">
    <Description>
        <p>
            Settings that every chart in the app shares (culture, time zone, height, legend, axes) go in a <code>RioChartDefaults</code>
            instead of on each chart. Register it once in <code>Program.cs</code> with <code>AddRioCharts</code>; that reaches every chart,
            whatever its render mode. Nothing else needs registering, and without it charts use their built-in defaults.
        </p>
        <p>
            To give part of the app other defaults, cascade a <code>RioChartDefaults</code> around it: each setting comes from the chart
            itself, else the cascade, else the registered defaults. A cascade without <code>IsFixed</code> can be replaced at runtime,
            e.g. on a language switch, and the charts follow. A cascade does not reach into components with another render mode, so prefer
            <code>AddRioCharts</code> for app-wide settings. A chart's <code>YAxis</code> replaces the default axis as a whole. Here both
            charts get the Swedish culture, 200 px and a legend at the bottom.
        </p>
    </Description>
    <ChildContent>
        <CascadingValue Value="_defaults" IsFixed="true">
            <RioChart TItem="Reading" Title="Sessions">
                <LineSeries Items="WebSessions" Name="Web" XValue="r => r.Time" YValue="r => r.Value" />
                <LineSeries Items="AppSessions" Name="App" XValue="r => r.Time" YValue="r => r.Value" />
            </RioChart>
            <RioChart TItem="Reading" Title="Sessions, total" YAxis="@(_defaults.YAxis! with { Title = "Total" })">
                <AreaSeries Items="WebSessions" Name="Web" XValue="r => r.Time" YValue="r => r.Value" Stack="all" />
                <AreaSeries Items="AppSessions" Name="App" XValue="r => r.Time" YValue="r => r.Value" Stack="all" />
            </RioChart>
        </CascadingValue>
    </ChildContent>
</Example>

<h2>How Blazor charts update</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Selectors run once per data change, never per render.</li>
    <li>A series reprocesses its data when <code>Items</code> or <code>Data</code> is a new reference, or <code>DataVersion</code> changes. Changing a list in place does not update the chart; assign a new list or bump <code>DataVersion</code>.</li>
    <li><code>YValue</code> returns <code>double?</code>; <code>null</code> leaves a gap. Cast <code>decimal</code> to <code>double</code>.</li>
    <li>The chart fills its container's width and is 300 px high by default (<code>Height</code>).</li>
</ul>

<h2 id="render-modes">Blazor render modes</h2>
<ul>
    <li>
        <strong>Interactive pages</strong> (Server, WebAssembly or Auto) get everything: tooltips, zoom, keyboard navigation and downloads.
    </li>
    <li>
        <strong>Static server rendering and prerendering</strong> draw the chart as plain SVG, with no JS. It is laid out at <code>Width</code>
        (or 640 px) and scales to its container. Tooltips, the download button and keyboard navigation appear once the page is interactive.
        Canvas series are drawn as SVG until then, so the prerendered chart is never blank.
    </li>
    <li>
        <strong>.NET MAUI Blazor Hybrid</strong> (<code>dotnet new maui-blazor</code>): the same package and usings, nothing else. The
        chart runs in the app's WebView and is always interactive. Tooltips, zoom, clicks and the PNG download work on Windows;
        Android and iOS have not been tested yet.
    </li>
</ul>

<h2 id="aot">Trimming and native AOT</h2>
<p>
    Every package is marked AOT-compatible and publishes without trimming warnings. JSON definitions use source-generated serialization,
    so Blazor WebAssembly, trimmed servers and native AOT apps (also with Skia export and the AI tools) work as they do in development.
    Read definitions with <code>ChartDefinitionJson.Parse</code>; it fills in defaults that the generated serializer cannot.
</p>

@code {
    private sealed record Package(string Id, string Text);

    private static readonly Package[] Packages =
    [
        new("RioCharts.Core", "The chart engine alone, with no UI dependencies."),
        new("RioCharts.Blazor", "The <RioChart> component for Blazor apps."),
        new("RioCharts.Skia", "PNG and SVG export on the server."),
        new("RioCharts.AI", "create_chart and render_chart tools for AI agents."),
        new("RioCharts.Mcp", "riocharts-mcp, a local MCP server for agents (a .NET tool)."),
    ];

    private const string AgentsCode = """
        ## Charts

        Charts use RioCharts.Blazor (1.0 release candidate, .NET 10 and 11). Before writing chart code, read
        https://riocharts.com/llms-full.txt, or call get_usage if the riocharts MCP server is set up.

        - Setup: the RioCharts.Blazor package, and `@using RioCharts.Blazor` and `@using RioCharts.Core` in `_Imports.razor`. Nothing to register.
        - Set `TItem` on `<RioChart>` when it has event handlers (`OnPointClick`, ...).
        - A chart updates when `Items` is a new list or `DataVersion` changes, not when a list is changed in place.
        - Tooltips, zoom and clicks need an interactive render mode on the page.
        """;

    private const string InstallCode = """
        dotnet add package RioCharts.Core --prerelease
        dotnet add package RioCharts.Blazor --prerelease
        dotnet add package RioCharts.Skia --prerelease
        dotnet add package RioCharts.AI --prerelease
        dotnet tool install -g RioCharts.Mcp --prerelease
        """;

    private static readonly AxisOptions Sessions = new() { Title = "Sessions" };
    private static readonly SeriesData _columns = Columns();

    private static SeriesData Columns()
    {
        var (times, values) = Big(20_000);
        return SeriesData.From(times, values);
    }

    private const string BlazorPathCode = """
        <RioChart Items="orders">
            <LineSeries XValue="r => r.Time" YValue="r => r.Value" />
        </RioChart>
        """;

    private const string ServerPathCode = """
        // dotnet add package RioCharts.Skia --prerelease
        var chart = new ChartDefinition
        {
            Title = "Orders per day",
            Series = [new SeriesOptions { Id = "orders", Name = "Orders" }],   // a line, unless Type says otherwise
        };
        var data = new Dictionary<string, SeriesData> { ["orders"] = SeriesData.From(days, orders) };

        byte[] png = ChartExport.ToPng(chart, data, new ExportOptions { Width = 640, Height = 320 });
        string svg = ChartExport.ToSvg(chart, data);
        """;

    private const string JsonPathCode = """"
        // RioCharts.Core reads and binds definitions; using RioCharts.Core.Definitions
        var json = """{"dataset":"sales","series":[{"type":"bar","x":"month","y":"revenue"}]}""";
        var datasets = new Dictionary<string, ChartDataset>
        {
            ["sales"] = ChartDataset.FromCsv("sales", File.ReadAllText("sales.csv")),
        };

        var definition = ChartDefinitionJson.Parse(json).Definition!;

        // In Blazor:  <RioChart Definition="definition" Datasets="datasets" />
        // On the server, with RioCharts.Skia:
        var bound = ChartDefinitionBinder.Bind(definition, datasets);
        byte[] png = ChartExport.ToPng(bound.Definition, bound.Data);
        """";

    private const string AiPathCode = """
        // Your own agent: dotnet add package RioCharts.AI --prerelease
        var tool = new ChartTool(new InMemoryDataSource([shop]));
        var response = await chatClient.GetResponseAsync("Chart orders per channel this week",
            new ChatOptions { Tools = tool.AsAIFunctions() });

        // Local agents (Claude Code, Copilot, ...): a .NET tool that charts CSV and JSON files
        // dotnet tool install -g RioCharts.Mcp --prerelease
        // .mcp.json: { "mcpServers": { "riocharts": { "command": "riocharts-mcp", "args": ["--data", "data"] } } }
        """;

    // "@" is '@': a line starting with @using inside @code would be read as a Razor directive.
    private const string ImportsCode = "@using RioCharts.Blazor\n@using RioCharts.Core";

    private const string FirstCode = """
        <RioChart Items="orders">
            <LineSeries XValue="r => r.Time" YValue="r => r.Value" />
        </RioChart>

        @code {
            record Reading(DateTime Time, double? Value);
            List<Reading> orders = ...; // orders per day
        }
        """;

    private static readonly RioChartDefaults _defaults = new()
    {
        Culture = System.Globalization.CultureInfo.GetCultureInfo("sv-SE"),
        Height = 200,
        Legend = new LegendOptions { Position = LegendPosition.Bottom },
        YAxis = new AxisOptions { Format = "#,0" },
    };

    private const string DefaultsCode = """
        // Program.cs: every chart in the app gets these (optional; works in every render mode)
        builder.Services.AddRioCharts(new RioChartDefaults
        {
            Culture = CultureInfo.GetCultureInfo("sv-SE"),
            TimeZone = "Europe/Stockholm",
            Height = 200,
            Legend = new LegendOptions { Position = LegendPosition.Bottom },
            YAxis = new AxisOptions { Format = "#,0" },
        });

        @* A section with other defaults: cascade them (each setting falls back to the registered ones) *@
        <CascadingValue Value="@(new RioChartDefaults { Height = 400 })" IsFixed="true">
            ...
        </CascadingValue>

        @* A page: its own YAxis wins (with keeps the default's other settings) *@
        @inject RioChartDefaults ChartDefaults
        <RioChart TItem="Reading" YAxis="@(ChartDefaults.YAxis! with { Title = "Total" })"> ... </RioChart>
        """;

    private const string SeveralCode = """
        <RioChart TItem="Reading" YAxis="@(new AxisOptions { Title = "Sessions" })">
            <LineSeries Items="web" Name="Web" XValue="r => r.Time" YValue="r => r.Value" />
            <LineSeries Items="app" Name="App" XValue="r => r.Time" YValue="r => r.Value" />
        </RioChart>
        """;

    private const string ArraysCode = """
        <RioChart TItem="double">
            <LineSeries Name="Response time" Data="SeriesData.From(times, values)" StrokeWidth="1.25" />
        </RioChart>
        """;
}
